TI  - A Digital Twin-Enhanced Simulation Approach for Smart Warehousing System Design and Evaluation
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 3rd International Conference on Design Science, ICDS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICDS67121.2025.11425240
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036163690?origin=resultslist
AB  - The design of modern smart warehouses is an inherently complex and challenging task. Decisions regarding automation levels, resource allocation, and operational strategies demand substantial investment and carry significant risk. This paper introduces the Digital Twin-Enhanced Simulation Approach, a systematic methodology for designing and evaluating smart warehousing systems. This approach integrates physical entity modeling, virtual process simulation, and dynamic data interaction to form a robust decision-support framework. It allows stakeholders to configure various warehouse designs - from manual to fully robotic - and assess their performance under diverse operational scenarios. To achieve this, we simulated 64 scenarios to generate diverse order profiles, then analyzed system performance against order concentration, as measured by the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI). The results show how our approach can quantify system efficiency, identify bottlenecks, and define the applicability boundaries for different automation levels based on this key index. This methodology provides a low-cost, data-driven tool for enterprises to optimize warehouse design, mitigate investment risk, and improve logistical agility. © 2025 IEEE.
