TI  - Polyglot: An LLM-Driven Semantic Control Plane for Cross-Format Type Interoperability Across Lakehouse Formats
PY  - 2026
T2  - FORMATS 2026 - Proceedings of the 1st International Workshop on Data FORMATS for Modern Architectures and Workloads
DO  - 10.1145/3802514.3809176
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042260945?origin=resultslist
AB  - The modern data lakehouse rests on the fragile assumption of semantic transparency across N compute engines and M storage and table formats. In practice, this O (N × M) integration space is governed by an "Interoperability Tax": manual, engine-specific adapters that fail to preserve semantic fidelity during dialect evolution. This fragmentation leads to Stochastic Signal Loss in ML pipelines and Logical Erasure in analytical queries, as existing schema registries enforce syntactic structure but remain oblivious to behavioral type drift.We present Polyglot, a framework that re-establishes Physical Independence by shifting interoperability from manual imperative code to a generative, versioned Mapping Manifest. Decomposing dialects into a 3-Tier Polyglot Intermediate Representation (PIR) reduces integration complexity to O(N + M), while a consensus-driven Agent-To-Agent (A2A) synthesis loop resolves nuanced type behaviors where single models fail. Empirical evaluations across five production dialects at LinkedIn ground manifest entries in observed engine behavior, surfacing semantic divergences invisible to traditional tooling. The result: Automated manifests that maintain transitive parity, transforming interoperability into a verifiable, manifest-driven contract. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
