TI  - RT-AIBI: A Real-Time AI-Augmented Business Intelligence Framework Leveraging Cloud Data Lakes for Agile Decision-Making
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 2nd Asia Pacific Conference on Innovation in Technology, APCIT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/APCIT65661.2025.11411230
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035596515?origin=resultslist
AB  - In the evolving landscape of digital enterprises, agility in decision-making is contingent on real-time insights derived from complex, large-scale datasets. This study introduces RT-AIBI, a Real-Time AI-Augmented Business Intelligence system that unifies cloud data lakes with smart analytics to support quick, precise, and situationally aware business decision making. The main modules are StreamFusion-Net, a latency- and heterogeneity-aware real-time data ingestion engine; InsightTransformer-BI, Transformer-based contextual prediction model; and AQO, an Adaptive Query Optimizer based on Reinforcement Learning that personalizes query execution plan to shorten delays. The information is consumed by a variety of sources such as transactional systems, IoT sensors, APIs, and social media, stored in a cloud-native Delta Lake that has ACID compliance and schema evolution. Experimental tests indicate substantial performance improvement: the ingestion latency of RT-AIBI can be reduced by up to 35% versus Kafka/Kinesis pipelines, query execution up to more than 50% versus legacy and cost-based optimizers. The model is achieved on InsightTransformer-BI with an average performance of 93.4 % accuracy on the test set better than 4.5-5.2 % in LSTM and Random Forest baselines on various tasks including salesoline forecasting, churn prediction or demand estimation. The dashboard performance is improved by 60%, facilitating interactive, agile data exploration. The architecture of RT-AIBI provides the following characteristics: scalability, the ability to have low latency analytics, and semantic alignment to business contexts. This qualifies it to work well with dynamic fields such as retail, fintech, and supply chain management where real-time intelligence is paramount. The research highlights the possibility of the combination of AI, cloud-native infrastructure and intelligent query planning to alter current knowledge about business intelligence systems. © 2025 IEEE.
