TI  - A COMPARATIVE STUDY OF DATA GOVERNANCE IN DATA FABRIC AND DATA MESH ARCHITECTURES
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 International Annual Conference and 46th Annual Meeting: Powering the Future of Engineering Management, ASEM 2025
SP  - 775
EP  - 784
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035378753?origin=resultslist
AB  - As organizations generate and manage increasing volumes of data, ensuring data quality and governance has become critical. Two modern architectures, Data Fabric and Data Mesh, offer distinct approaches to managing and governing data in complex, distributed environments. While both aim to enhance accessibility, trust, and interoperability, they differ in governance structures, policy enforcement, and operational efficiency. Data Fabric provides a centralized governance model that integrates data across diverse sources using automation, metadata management, and AI-driven quality controls. This approach ensures consistency, compliance, and real-time data observability, making it ideal for organizations seeking uniform governance policies across enterprise-wide data assets. However, its reliance on centralized control can create bottlenecks and reduce agility in rapidly changing data environments. In contrast, Data Mesh adopts a decentralized, domain-oriented governance model, treating data as a product owned by specific business domains. It empowers domain teams with autonomy while enforcing a federated governance framework to maintain global standards. This approach enhances agility and scalability but requires strong coordination mechanisms to ensure data consistency, security, and interoperability across domains. This paper compares these architectures, evaluating their impact on data quality, and governance. It provides insights into the trade-offs, challenges, and best practices for implementing these frameworks based on an organization's strategic needs. By understanding these approaches, businesses can design more effective governance models to ensure trustworthy and high-quality data. © American Society for Engineering Management, 2025.
