TI  - Securing The Cognitive Enterprise: Zero-Trust Agentic Knowledge Retention in Cyber-Physical Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 7th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Robotics, and Control, AIRC 2026
SP  - 421
EP  - 427
DO  - 10.1109/AIRC69745.2026.11631472
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047328847?origin=resultslist
AB  - The manufacturing and construction sectors, including Industrial Cyber-Physical Systems (ICPSs), are undergoing a significant digital transformation, resulting in the generation of large-scale, heterogeneous data streams from Internet of Things (IoT) devices, Building Information Modeling (BIM) platforms, and operational systems. These data embody critical tacit and explicit knowledge vulnerable to loss through siloing, attrition, and cyber threats. This paper proposes a novel integrated Knowledge Management (KM) strategy in a conceptual framework that synergizes Agentic AI with a zero-trust security paradigm to automate knowledge discovery and ensure long-term resilient retention. The core innovation is a cyclical 'Agentic Knowledge' architecture, built upon a multi-modal data lakehouse, a semantic discovery engine powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) and knowledge graphs, with a tiered retention system. We perform a structural analysis, detailing the implementation of vector embeddings for semantic search, graph neural networks for relational inference, and differential privacy to emphasize security. The framework explicitly embeds cyber-awareness through zero-trust access controls, adversarial resilience mechanisms, and sandboxed execution environments that isolate agentic workloads to contain potential runtime compromises. A comparative analysis justifies the shift from traditional, passive KM tools to a dynamic, AI-augmented stack. We present key performance indicators (KPIs) for validation and discuss expected findings, with a significant reduction in information retrieval time with mitigation of knowledge atrophy.  © 2026 IEEE.
