AU  - Srivastava, A.
TI  - Legacy ETL to Autonomous Data Clouds: AI's Role in Modern Data Warehouse Transformation
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence for Sustainable Engineering and Innovation, AISEI 2026
SP  - 238
EP  - 244
DO  - 10.1109/AISEI68628.2026.11572729
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044310688?origin=resultslist
AB  - Legacy ETL systems were built with the requirements of batch-oriented, rigid data processing in mind, which made them less adaptable to supporting new-age real-time analytics, multi-structured data, and AI-based decisioning systems. Cloud-native data platforms, augmented by AI and machine learning, have ushered in a new era of autonomous data clouds that offer the benefits of self-optimizing pipelines, adaptive schema evolution, automated anomaly detection, and smart workload management. This paper explores the technological transition from legacy ETL systems to an AI-governed, autonomous data environment. This paper also presents a new reference architecture that brings forth the benefits of AI-enabled ETL automation, medallion transformation, autonomous data management, and real-time analytic acceleration. Through a real-time business use case, the paper will present concrete benefits of using cloud-native AI-native cloud warehouses, which will drive new-age transformation in the data strategy of all businesses. Cloud-native data platform innovation has accelerated this transformation from traditional ETL-centric architectures to self-optimizing data clouds that support autonomous ingestion, transformation, governance, and analysis. The traditional ETL process architecture, which is inflexible, human-enabled in its orchestration, and deeply coupled with on-premises systems, falls short of supporting the emerging, adaptive, and scalable needs of enterprises that require real-time capabilities. This paper explores the importance of Artificial Intelligence in modernizing data warehouse infrastructure by enabling AI-enabled data schema mapping, autonomic data quality enforcement, adaptive orchestration, and data warehouse-centric optimization, supporting an effortless transition from traditional ETL-centric architectures to self-optimizing data clouds. This research proposes a comprehensive data platform framework that leverages Artificial Intelligence components to detect data schema changes, develop transformation rules, optimize execution workloads, and enforce governance in decentralized data clouds. This study clearly confirms that data clouds augmented with Artificial Intelligence technologies enable autonomous, faster execution of data platforms, ensuring enterprises faster time-to-insight and improved data reliability at reduced operational costs. This paper clearly establishes the prospective importance of Artificial Intelligence in the innovation and transformation of next-generation data war. © 2026 IEEE.
