TI  - From Report Painter to SAP Business Data Cloud: A Technical Evolution of SAP Analytics Solutions Review
PY  - 2025
T2  - 1st IEEE International Conference on Data Science and Intelligent Network Computing, ICDSINC 2025
SP  - 801
EP  - 810
DO  - 10.1109/ICDSINC66221.2025.11448162
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037443997?origin=resultslist
AB  - Over the past three decades, SAP's analytics landscape has evolved through successive architectural transformations, each addressing specific scalability, performance, and business agility challenges. This study presents a structured review of that evolution, beginning with the early enterprise resource planning (ERP)-embedded reporting tools such as the Logistics Information System (LIS), Executive Information System (EIS), and Profitability Analysis (CO-PA). It then traces the development through the SAP Business Warehouse (BW) era, the in-memory revolution introduced by SAP HANA, and the emergence of cloud-native platforms including SAP Data Warehouse Cloud, Datasphere, and ultimately SAP Business Data Cloud (BDC). The analysis emphasizes key technological milestones, such as the separation of OLTP and OLAP workloads, the introduction of in-memory columnar processing, and the adoption of hybrid and federated models, while examining their implications for data governance, system performance, and user experience. By synthesizing technical documentation and historical case evidence, this paper establishes a framework for understanding how SAP analytics solutions have evolved from tightly coupled ERP reports to context-aware, AI-ready data fabrics that enable real-time, enterprise-wide decision intelligence. © 2025 IEEE.
