TI  - From Data Governance to Contract-Constrained AI Systems: Architecture and Evaluation for Enterprise NL-to-SQL with AI-Ready Data Models
PY  - 2026
T2  - IAIT 2026 - 14th International Conference on Advances in Information Technology
C7  - 53
DO  - 10.1145/3816713.3819785
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045239505?origin=resultslist
AB  - Enterprises are using natural language interfaces that turn user questions into SQL to make analytics available to more people. While large language models (LLMs) often produce grammatically correct SQL, real-world use shows ongoing issues with meaning, context, and the reliability of answers. Traditional data warehouses, semantic layers, and governance tools were made for human analysts working in controlled reporting settings. They do not clearly include rules for using AI or requirements for checking the information. This paper is based on real findings from an enterprise AI Data Assistant that was tested on 3000 queries. It highlights common issues in NL-to-SQL interaction, such as semantic drift, context collapse, verification gaps, and boundary ambiguity. We demonstrate that just because SQL can be executed does not mean it is correct for business use. Metadata confusion and unclear semantic definitions are key factors that lead to these failures. This work introduces a layered AI-Ready Data Model and an AI-Expanded Data Contract framework. This framework formalizes hybrid reference anchors, including KPI, domain, request class, and question template-based anchors, as well as mandatory verification requirements. We further formalize the gap between executability and business correctness and describe a contract-constrained NL-to-SQL architecture that integrates usage constraints and verification into the query pipeline. Evidence shows that executable SQL rate remains consistently high at approximately 99%, while business correctness improves from 54% to 68% after introducing contract-constrained mechanisms. We also discovered 82 terms that were not previously documented in the business glossary through AI interaction. Reliable enterprise AI therefore requires not only better models, but also data governed by explicit contracts and evaluated beyond syntactic success.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
