TI  - Fast, Fault-Tolerant, and Affordable: A Modern Data Lakehouse ETL Pipeline for Emerging Economies
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 28th International Conference on Computer and Information Technology, ICCIT 2025
SP  - 3440
EP  - 3445
DO  - 10.1109/ICCIT68739.2025.11491349
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041629696?origin=resultslist
AB  - Today, enterprises generate massive volumes of semi-structured data from IoT devices, applications, and web services. Traditional ETL systems struggle with such workloads due to rigid schemas, limited scalability, and weak fault tolerance, slowing analytics and machine learning. This paper presents an automated data lakehouse pipeline integrating MongoDB, Apache Spark, Apache Airflow, MinIO S3, and Apache Iceberg. The system extracts data from MongoDB, performs recursive type conversions and schema normalization using Spark, and stores them as Iceberg tables on MinIO. Airflow automates and monitors workflow execution with scheduling and retry support, ensuring robustness and reproducibility. Experiments show notable improvements over Hadoop-based ETL: ingestion 3 × faster, ETL time reduced by 64%, query latency decreased by 68%, and CPU usage lowered by 30%. The pipeline guaranties ACID compliance, supports schema evolution, and integrates with Trino, Superset, and Kubeflow for analytics and machine learning workloads. Compared to cloud warehouses, the architecture is significantly more cost-efficient by eliminating licensing fees, avoiding idle-compute costs, and using low-cost object storage, resulting in lower Total Cost of Ownership (TCO). Overall, the proposed pipeline offers a scalable, fault-tolerant, and futureready solution for modern big data ecosystems. © 2025 IEEE.
