AB  - The proceedings contain 46 papers. The special focus in this conference is on New Trends in Databases and Information Systems. The topics include: Extending the Applicability of Bloom Filters by Relaxing Their Parameter Constraints; CoDD: A Constraint-Based Dataset Discovery Tool for Open Data Lakes; could More Be Less: The Case of Location(s) Awareness in Time Series Prediction; spatio-Temporal Data and Molecular Dynamics: Challenges and Opportunities (Vision Paper); From ER Conceptual Models to Document-Based NoSQL Logical Models; Querying Property Graphs with XPath; timeVizBench: An Interactive Platform for Evaluating Techniques for Efficient Large Time Series Visualization; decentralized Research Data Sharing Management Using Blockchain Technology; experiversum: An Environment for Curating Data-Driven Experimental Sciences; extension of Data Catalog Vocabulary for Federating Open Datasets in Data Spaces; Enhancing SQL Learning Through Generative AI and Student Error Analysis; GESONGEN: An Interface for Generating and Visualizing Geosocial Networks; Accelerating Entity Resolution Through Vectorized Meta-blocking on GPUs; validating Data Provenance Polynomials; data Governance Maturity Models and Practices: A Systematic Literature Review; an Evaluation of Energy Consumption for Deep Learning-Based Privacy Preserving Record Linkage; machine Learning for Query Optimization in Knowledge Graphs; vector Databases and Language Models: Synergies and Challenges; Ethical and Equitable Data Science: Bridging Social Justice and Technical Innovation ADBIS 2025 Doctoral Consortium Lecture; data Integration for Data Science: Solutions and Still Open Problems; enhancing Data Interoperability in Multi-platform Lakehouses with Apache Iceberg; Executable Semantics for Teaching Concatenative Stack-Based DSLs: The Case of StackLang; exploring Big Data Maturity Models: Findings from a Systematic Literature Review; improving Data Discovery Effectiveness: Experimental Evaluation o