TI  - Strengthening Data Quality in National Health Data Warehouses: A Systematic Review and Mixed-Methods Case Study from Uganda’s Digital Health Integration Experience
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 Symposium on Software Engineering in the Global South, SEiGS 2026
SP  - 71
EP  - 78
DO  - 10.1145/3786176.3788341
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047267982?origin=resultslist
AB  - Digital health systems have expanded rapidly worldwide, with over 85 percent of countries now adopting national digital health platforms. Africa has taken a particularly innovative approach, emphasizing open-source tools and “African solutions to African digital challenges.” Uganda’s digital health architecture reflects this evolution, combining DHIS2, UgandaEMR, eCHIS, and a national interoperability layer under the OpenHIE framework. To unify these data sources, the Ministry of Health established the National Health Data Warehouse (NHDW). However, as data moves across systems, new quality issues emerge. This study used a mixed-methods approach comprising a systematic review of global interoperability-related data quality evidence; a case study of the NHDW; quantitative analysis using the WHO Data Quality Review (DQR) methodology on 2024–2025 datasets; and qualitative insights from 18 key informant interviews including NHDW developers, MoH experts, and regional biostatisticians. The results show clear degradation of data during interoperability: record-level discrepancies of 10–25 percent, duplication rates of 5–20 percent, missing values due to mapping issues, and synchronization delays of up to seven days. KIIs highlighted metadata fragmentation, weak automation, inconsistent patient identifiers, and infrastructure challenges at facility and community levels as major contributors. The study concludes that interoperability magnifies systemic weaknesses, but also provides a unique opportunity to strengthen national data governance. Recommendations include a national metadata authority, FHIR-based canonical data models, automated validation engines, improved frontline infrastructure, and real-time quality dashboards. These interventions are essential for Uganda and other LMICs striving to achieve trusted, interoperable, and high-quality digital health systems. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
