TI  - A novel Blockchain-based Access Control Manager to Electronic Health Records (EHRs)
PY  - 2021
T2  - Blockchain for Smart Cities
SP  - 233
EP  - 244
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-824446-3.00003-X
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85127189433?origin=resultslist
AB  - The main objective of this chapter is to propose a novel blockchain-based access control manager to Electronic Health Records. Our system gives patients a complete, immutable log and seamless access to their medical information across healthcare providers. The proposed system facilitates authentication, authorization, confidentiality, privacy-preserving, and data sharing in crucial conditions when handling sensitive information. Blockchain access control manager integrates with providers’ existing, local data storage, facilitating interoperability, and making our system convenient and flexible. The proposed system motivates medical stakeholders (patients, doctors, healthcare providers, pharma industry, insurance companies, and researchers) to participate in the network as a blockchain entity. The blockchain access control manager would facilitate continuous monitoring of high-risk patients that would notify caregivers and health providers if a patient reached a critical threshold for action. Caregivers could reach out to the patient and coordinate treatment options for early intervention. The main objective of this chapter is to provide a working protocol for blockchain-based automated access control managers that do not require trust in a third party instead it is going to execute instructions and queries such as storing, retrieval, and data sharing through which we analyze and discuss our approach. © 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
