TI  - Integrate Blockchain and Federated Learning Approaches to Secure Multi-Cloud Data Exchange: An AI-Powered Trust Management System
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 5th International Conference on Emerging Research in Electronics, Computer Science and Technology, ICERECT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICERECT65215.2025.11378085
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034838181?origin=resultslist
AB  - Multi-cloud environments have emerged as one of the leading methods to manage large-scale, distributed and diverse-situated heterogeneous data across different industries (healthcare, finance, smart cities) in the age of digital transformation. On the other hand, this widespread exchange of data across cloud boundaries, also known as Data Mesh has unleashed large security and trust issues. Federated Learning (FL) is a decentralized machine learning paradigm that incorporates the privacy-preserving technique, allowing multiple parties to train AI models cooperatively without having to reveal the raw data. While FL offers several advantages due to decentralized training, it still faces some challenges like poisoning attacks, trust management and lack of accountability across clouds. Thus, this research provides an integrated framework combining Federated Learning and Blockchain technology for the first time rendering a stable, transparent, and AI infested trust management system among multi-cloud data transaction while countervailing all these impediments. The architecture proposes using the immutability and decentralized consensus of Blockchain to validate and record trust scores, model updates, as well as participant behavior in a federated network. Smart contracts are used to enforce fine-grained access control dynamically and incentivize data providers and cloud nodes to behave honestly. To train models across various data silos, we use Federated Learning and deploy an AI-driven trust engine that continuously assesses the credibility of nodes based on behavioral analytics as well as quality and historical performance of contribution. This framework guarantees data sovereignty, integrity and auditability while still preserving user privacy. Extensive simulations and empirical analysis show that the proposed system can effectively defend against adversarial threats (e. g., model poisoning, unauthorized access, data tampering). In addition, the federation system was significantly improved about integrity and performance under heterogeneous data and cloud environments with AI models integrated trust-aware functionality. The framework also enables dynamic trust reconfiguration based on emerging threats or policy changes, thereby ensuring long-term scalability and resilience. This research hence serves as a very strong model towards trustworthy multi-cloud ecosystems, closing the gap between secure AI collaboration and transparent data governance. © 2025 IEEE.
