AB  - Reliance on human-in-the-loop decision-making severely constrains the scalability of coordinated agent action in high volume commercial, civil, and military operations. In large scale military theatres of operation, adversarial threats pose additional challenges on distributed command and control (C2). Increasing the capability of autonomous systems to meet these challenges across multi-mission environments requires rapid and resilient distribution of actionable intelligence. To achieve decentralized C2 at scale, a corresponding system-of-systems relies on an integrated 'fabric' of mission information and reasoning services. One critical enabler of this emergent capability is its foundational communications and networking infrastructure. This paper explores the use of heterogeneous hardened 5G-based radio access networks as the edge transport layer of data and reasoning fabrics. The proposed multi-domain service framework enables low latency edge and peer-to-peer decision making across a large set of tightly coordinated agents. A tactical uncrewed aerial system (UAS) mission is selected as a use case to illustrate data and reasoning fabric exchange of critical micro-and macro-weather intelligence. Further consideration is offered for Joint All Domain Command and Control (JADC2) and Advanced Battlefield Management Systems (ABMS) missions requiring faster, more secure, and more accurate communications channels for complex operational needs.  © 2022 IEEE.
