TI  - Serverless Data Lake Architecture Using BigQuery and Cloud Functions
PY  - 2025
T2  - ICATEI 2025 - International Conference on Advanced Technologies in Energy and Informatic
SP  - 91
EP  - 96
DO  - 10.1109/ICATEI67676.2025.11404917
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036819384?origin=resultslist
AB  - This paper presents a modular, serverless architecture for building scalable data lakes using Google BigQuery and Cloud Functions. Traditional data lake implementations often require significant infrastructure management, scheduled ETL processes, and manual scaling, which can hinder agility and increase operational costs. In contrast, the proposed approach leverages fully managed Google Cloud services to ingest, transform, and query data in real time with minimal configuration and overhead. The architecture integrates Cloud Storage for raw data landing, Cloud Functions for event-driven processing, Pub or Sub for asynchronous messaging, and BigQuery for analytics. Automation and orchestration are enhanced using Cloud Scheduler and Cloud Run. We evaluate the performance of this serverless pipeline through a benchmark study against a Hadoop-based architecture, focusing on ingestion latency, system scalability, operational cost, and fault tolerance. Experimental results show a significant reduction in latency (up to 7 X), over 60% cost savings, and automatic handling of ingestion bursts without human intervention. The system also demonstrates strong fault resilience through retry mechanisms and integrated monitoring. These findings highlight the effectiveness of serverless architectures for modern data lake deployments and provide a blueprint for organizations seeking to modernize their data platforms with low-maintenance, cloudnative solutions.  © 2025 IEEE.
