AB  - Biological databases have become valuable resources in the field of systems biology. They allow us to store, integrate, and analyze various types of omics data. This explores their origins, discusses database architecture strategies, examines the integration of omics data, addresses key challenges in database curation and management, and delves into the future possibilities involving machine learning and artificial intelligence. Throughout the chapter, we highlight how databases have advanced from data repositories to platforms that support complex queries, data federation, and analytical capabilities. Some of the challenges we discuss include ensuring data quality, promoting interoperability among databases, achieving semantic coherence across datasets, maintaining security, and incorporating robust analytical functionalities. Ultimately this chapter emphasizes the role that biological databases play in synthesizing knowledge from high-throughput experiments and applying it to bioinformatics research. It offers insights into the achievements of current and future contributions of databases, to data-intensive systems biology research. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
