AU  - Gupta, S.
AU  - Vanaparthi, N.R.
AU  - Kumar, S.N.P.
TI  - AI-Enabled Decentralized Data Mesh for Scalable Cybersecurity and Real-Time Compliance in Financial Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems
VL  - 1953 LNNS
SP  - 301
EP  - 313
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-24929-6_25
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045701523?origin=resultslist
AB  - As regulatory expectations increase and financial services ecosystems become more vulnerable to cyber attacks, financial institutions are struggling to deal with large and disparate datasets. The proliferation of available data and digital assets is overwhelming, as is the regulatory requests to report on such data and digital assets. Traditional centralized data architecture creates siloed data ownership, slow interoperability and few options for flexibility resulting in late delivery of predictability, detection of fraud, or even just satisfy regulatory timelines. The security paradigms leave existing financial institutions ill-prepared to establish the necessary digital transformation to defend against sophisticated and distributed attacks in the financial services ecosystem. This paper, therefore, proposes an interoperable framework to resolve these issues through a comprehensive model to combine AI -based cyber security mesh frameworks, with the decentralized Data Mesh concepts best suited in the financial space. In the proposed framework, data as a product will be owned by business domains, self serve data platforms, and federated governance with a combination of autonomy and compliance from regulation. The proposed framework is intended to be a holistic model working with transformer risk models and distributed participating artificial-intelligent agents to detect anomalies in real-time and automate compliance with the aggregated group of metadata driven policy rules and interoperability standards. Data Mesh and AI-powered cybersecurity mesh will improve efficiency and timeliness of security and improve compliance for financial institutions. Some of the areas of improvement include better accuracy of attack detections (improvements from 84.1% to 96.2%), faster threat mitigation speed (600 ms), faster customer data access (48 h to implement change to 2 h), and operational flexibility resulting from distributed data ownership. The proposed solution will reduce the data access latency, improve the accuracy of predictions, and improve the management of fraud and false positives, facilitating a pathway for legacy systems to migrate to scalable compliance and improve the management of financial data and modernization of cybersecurity. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
