AB  - In this study, the authors proposed a computing framework that combined a federated learning system based on blockchain technology for preventing denial-of-service (DoS) and distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) intrusion on the organization electronic healthcare (e-healthcare) data warehouse infrastructure. The attack involves the injection of false data from a remote location, and data collected from an internet of things (IoT) node is gathered by a Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) broker, which forwards the data to the computing platform for analysis. In the digital health sector, protecting sensitive patient medical data is a top priority for healthcare professionals. Healthcare data regulations like General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) imposes legal duties on the healthcare administrators to safeguard patient data and notify patients of any breaches. Due to the severe consequences for noncompliance, stringent data collecting, privacy protection, and cybersecurity enforcement are required to stop breaches. This study accomplished two important goals on setting the proposed strategy apart from every existing methods. At first, we combined blockchain technology with federated learning system to create a cognitive computing paradigm that improved accuracy that made fake data injection attacks impossible, and provided an incentive system for data mining and data warehouse record validation. In the second method, we introduced secured MQTT communication as a gatekeeper strategy to prevent indiscriminate node flooding by enabling selective client admittance by MQTT broker via the MQTT protocol, which then forwards their message to the broker node for verification and authentication using Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) blockchain consensus algorithm. © 2026 A. Jose Anand and Saravanan Krishnan.
