AB  - This research proposes EcoTrend, a system designed to address the fragmentation of critical data (rainfall, air quality and vegetation index) across various platforms. The objective is to create a single unified intelligence platform for integrated environmental health assessment and decision support in a specific region. Interpreting multiple environmental indicators separately often leads to fragmented insights, making it difficult for stakeholders to assess overall ecohealth accurately. A composite metric is therefore essential to simplify complex datasets, enable region-toregion comparison, and support timely, data-driven environmental decisions. EcoTrend is conceptualized around a Medallion Data Architecture (Bronze-SilverGold). This structure systematically collects heterogeneous data from diverse sources - such as OpenMeteo for rainfall, WAQI/OWM for air quality, and MODIS via Google Earth Engine for vegetation - and centralizes it in a PostgreSQL data warehouse. The platform will employ both descriptive and predictive analytical models. Descriptive analytics will focus on visualizing historical patterns, geospatial heatmaps, and correlations (e.g., rainfall vs. Air Quality Index). For forecasting, predictive models Prophet and Regression will be used for short-term predictions of air rainfall and vegetation. A key innovation is the EcoHealth Composite Index, which integrates multiple environmental indicators into a single, easily interpretable score. The final output is an interactive dashboard, presenting real-time metrics, comprehensive historical analysis and forecasts. By providing policymakers, researchers and communities with actionable insights and alerts. EcoTrend aims to foster informed decision-making, thereby enhancing regional environmental sustainability and public well-being. © 2026 IEEE.
