AB  - In the original PDF of this article, due to a production error several direct quotations from key sources were omitted from the published version of this article. The omitted content is provided below, organized by the section in which it was intended to appear. 1. Introduction—First Paragraph The first paragraph of the Introduction section should read as follows: “Every year thousands of articles and dozens of books comment on the meaning of new computer technologies for people, organizations and society. Much of the literature about computing describes emerging technologies and the ways they expand the limits of the possible. Faster, tinier computers can make it easier for people to find information…The remarkable improvement in the capabilities of computing equipment from one decade to the next excites researchers, developers, and entrepreneurs, as well as the battalions of journalists who document these events in newspapers and magazines.” (Kling, 1996, p. 41). “Every year thousands of articles and dozens of books comment on the meaning of new computer technologies for people, organizations and society. Much of the literature about computing describes emerging technologies and the ways they expand the limits of the possible. Faster, tinier computers can make it easier for people to find information…The remarkable improvement in the capabilities of computing equipment from one decade to the next excites researchers, developers, and entrepreneurs, as well as the battalions of journalists who document these events in newspapers and magazines.” (Kling, 1996, p. 41). 2. Computerization Movement Section—Under “Vision” The following quotation should appear after the sentence: “Such utopianism, both optimistic and pessimistic, could bring potential challenges:” “Both the optimistic and the pessimistic stories of computerization are often crafted with the conventions of utopian and anti-utopian writing. Although utopian versions often serve important roles in giving people a positive sense of direction, they can mislead when their architects exaggerate the likelihood of easy and desirable social changes.” (Kling, 1996, p. 85). “Such utopianism, both optimistic and pessimistic, could bring potential challenges:” “Both the optimistic and the pessimistic stories of computerization are often crafted with the conventions of utopian and anti-utopian writing. Although utopian versions often serve important roles in giving people a positive sense of direction, they can mislead when their architects exaggerate the likelihood of easy and desirable social changes.” (Kling, 1996, p. 85). 3. AI as a Computerization Movement Section—Under “Trajectory and Visions” The following quotation should appear after the sentence: “Kling and Iacono (1988) commented on their observations of this movement:” “Media accounts usually describe the value of current technologies in terms of their potential capabilities. They often exaggerate their level of development, the quality of product engineering, and the extent to which they have been adopted. They have also exaggerated the attributes of the few commercial applications that do exist. They suggest that these systems "think" like people rather than simply performing symbolic manipulations on formal representations of the tasks usually performed by people. The American press often promotes AI in sensationalist terms[…]It heralds its cover story about AI with: "See machines think. Think, machine, think. The world of artificial intelligence." More accurate stories sometimes appear in magazines and newspapers, but they are less common than the sensationalized stories. Despite sensational claims in the press, none of the higher performance AI systems, with the exception of a particular checkers-playing program, has "learned" to modify its behavior, let alone learned the way that humans do.” (Kling and Iacono, 1988, p. 231). “Media accounts usually describe the value of current technologies in terms of their potential capabilities. They often exaggerate their level of development, the quality of product engineering, and the extent to which they have been adopted. They have also exaggerated the attributes of the few commercial applications that do exist. They suggest that these systems "think" like people rather than simply performing symbolic manipulations on formal representations of the tasks usually performed by people. The American press often promotes AI in sensationalist terms[…]It heralds its cover story about AI with: "See machines think. Think, machine, think. The world of artificial intelligence." More accurate stories sometimes appear in magazines and newspapers, but they are less common than the sensationalized stories. Despite sensational claims in the press, none of the higher performance AI systems, with the exception of a particular checkers-playing program, has "learned" to modify its behavior, let alone learned the way that humans do.” (Kling and Iacono, 1988, p. 231). 4. The Use of Terms in CMs Section The following quotations should appear after the sentence: “De Goes (2013) observed this phenomenon across various industries:” “Every so often a term becomes so beloved by the media that it moves from ‘instructive’ to ‘hackneyed’ to ‘worthless,’ and Big Data is one of those terms…”—Roger Ehrenberg. “Every product by every vendor supports big data… and every ‘industry leader’ with every talk needs to include the phrase in the title of their talk and repeat it as many times as possible. So every data warehouse pitch is rehashed as a big data pitch, every data governance, master data management, OLAP, data mining—everything is now big data.”—Rob Klopp.“Big data as a technological category is becoming an increasingly meaningless name.”—Barry Devlin. “Every so often a term becomes so beloved by the media that it moves from ‘instructive’ to ‘hackneyed’ to ‘worthless,’ and Big Data is one of those terms…”—Roger Ehrenberg. “Every product by every vendor supports big data… and every ‘industry leader’ with every talk needs to include the phrase in the title of their talk and repeat it as many times as possible. So every data warehouse pitch is rehashed as a big data pitch, every data governance, master data management, OLAP, data mining—everything is now big data.”—Rob Klopp. “Big data as a technological category is becoming an increasingly meaningless name.”—Barry Devlin. The original article has been updated. © The Author(s) 2025.
