TI  - Harnessing Large Language Models for Data Mesh Adoption in Multi-organizational Data Ecosystems
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems
VL  - 1940 LNNS
SP  - 389
EP  - 395
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-23684-5_44
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047673193?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid increase in the volume, variety, and velocity of data is driving organizations to turn their increasing amount of information into valuable insights. The data mesh paradigm, anchored in domain-oriented ownership, data-as-a-product principles, and federated governance, offers a viable approach. However, it also introduces new operational challenges in data integration, data quality management, interoperability processes, and the risk of data redundancy. This study explores the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to construct a virtual data mesh architecture, referred to as the LLM-Driven Virtual Data Mesh platform. Within this architecture, interoperable virtual data products are conceptualized as standardized replicas derived from those available in each domain. The framework outlines three modules: Semantic Mapping and Metadata Integration, Dark Data Integration, and Data Quality Management. In addition, ad hoc data products can be integrated in the virtual layer, termed New Data Products and Special Data Products. The discussion outlines advantages, limitations, and risks, highlighting how LLMs can be employed to construct virtual layers that prevent data duplication. The proposed shared virtual architecture could be adopted by multiple stakeholders to enhance analytical systems, with a use case explored as a potential prototype focused on sales forecasting based on heterogeneous data. Finally, future research directions are outlined, including the definition of heterogeneous benchmarks, quantitative explainability metrics, and strategies for managing the degradation of model performance in dynamic environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
