DO  - 10.1109/C366505.2025.11340371
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033339541?origin=resultslist
AB  - Traditional ETL processes face two critical challenges: the low scalability of sequential processing and the complexity of migrating business logic embedded in client-side data sources, such as spreadsheets. This paper presents an optimized architecture, based on microservices and asynchronous parallel processing, to address both problems. The solution implements a task queue with RabbitMQ to divide and process large volumes of data concurrently, achieving both horizontal and vertical scalability. Additionally, a parsing system is integrated that uses Abstract Syntax Trees (AST) and dependency graphs to interpret and translate the formulas contained in spreadsheets (.xlsx) into SQL, centralizing operations in the target database and migrating computational logic into the data warehouse. Performance tests demonstrate that parallel execution improves overall performance by up to 55 % and reduces validation times by 70 %, while the migration of formulas via parsing also decreases loading times by 9.7 %. The resulting architecture not only drastically accelerates data pipelines but also enhances governance and integrity by completely centralizing both validation rules and data transformation logic.  © 2025 IEEE.
