TI  - LLM-driven data lineage analysis for construction monitoring in large hydropower projects
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of SPIE - The International Society for Optical Engineering
VL  - 14241
C7  - 1424123
DO  - 10.1117/12.3115438
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041167254?origin=resultslist
AB  - To address the challenges of governing massive, dynamic, and multimodal monitoring data in large hydropower projects, this paper proposes a data lake-based and large language model (LLM)-driven data lineage analysis framework for Internet of Things (IoT) environments. By constructing a directed acyclic graph (DAG) to model data lineage and leveraging LLMs to automatically parse and annotate ETL processes, metadata, and execution logs, the framework enables semantic and structured end-to-end traceability of data processing workflows. Furthermore, multimodal embedding techniques are employed to jointly represent and retrieve textual records (e.g., construction logs and rules), time-series sensor data (e.g., temperature, humidity, and strain), and on-site images, significantly enhancing the explainability and contextual relevance of lineage query results. Users can directly issue natural language queries, such as identifying the causes of data anomalies or tracking changes in transformation rules, and the system interprets query intent using LLMs to automatically retrieve relevant lineage paths and multimodal evidence from the data lake. This provides construction management personnel with intuitive, reliable, and decision-oriented analytical support. Experiments conducted on real-world hydropower construction datasets demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves notable improvements in query accuracy, interpretability, and response efficiency. The results indicate that the proposed approach offers an effective solution for intelligent data governance in large hydropower projects and provides a foundation for data lineage analysis and explainable decision-making in broader smart engineering and industrial IoT scenarios. © 2026 SPIE.
