SP  - 323
EP  - 333
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-21524-6_27
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046323528?origin=resultslist
AB  - Europe’s digital sovereignty relies on controlling data, workloads, and critical infrastructure, without hindering innovation or performance. The paper proposes that a federated, multi-vendor cloud–edge continuum—managed collaboratively by hyperscalers, European providers, edge platforms, and telcos—is the pragmatic solution to achieve this sovereignty. The 8ra approach is based on principles such as federation by design, openness, portability, policy-driven orchestration, verifiable trust, and locality-aware autonomy. These are realized through reference architecture comprising a multi-domain control plane, a unified identity and policy layer, a data fabric that enforces residency and access requirements, and programmable connectivity for guaranteed end-to-end performance. By leveraging 8ra software, the architecture enables workload placement close to data while respecting legal constraints, supports portable deployments across various technologies, and offers verifiable security and compliance through tools like SBOMs and confidential computing. Pilot implementations show reduced vendor lock-in, faster compliance with EU regulations, and improved resilience and observability at the edge. The paper concludes with a governance model based on shared catalogs, reciprocal SLAs, and conformity assessments, providing a standards-based roadmap for Europe to implement digital sovereignty through a federated cloud-edge-telco ecosystem. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
