AB  - Advances in artificial intelligence have propelled research in education, fuelling innovative applications of AI that focus specifically on content creation and personalised learning experiences. However, current generative AI authoring tools lack the structural grounding necessary to produce culturally appropriate content. Yet refugee learners in fragile contexts, such as those in the Accelerated Education Program (AEP) in Uganda, require educational materials that are both pedagogically sound and culturally contextualised. This study presents a novel, semantic-aware multimodal data warehouse designed to ingest and govern crowdsourced artefacts. Moving beyond relational modelling, an ontology-driven knowledge graph is introduced that semantically links unstructured environmental realities to formal competency-based curriculum (CBC) standards. The system embeds strict data-governance and ethical safeguards, enforcing privacy-by-design, consent tracking, and cultural validation to protect vulnerable refugee populations. Evaluated on real-world data validated by 10 expert AEP teachers, the multi-agent AI system successfully retrieved verified local context and achieved 73.5% zero-shot accuracy in generating culturally aligned, hallucination-free educational scripts. This establishes a fully implemented, scalable data architecture that proves the viability of deploying culturally aligned, privacy-preserving AI in extremely low-resource educational environments.  © 2026 The Authors.
