SP  - 1967
EP  - 1971
DO  - 10.1093/jamia/ocac147
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85139483534?origin=resultslist
AB  - To facilitate the secondary usage of electronic health record data for research, the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF) recently implemented a clinical data warehouse including, among other data, deidentified clinical notes and reports, which are available to UCSF researchers without Institutional Review Board approval. For deidentification of these notes, most of the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act identifiers are redacted, but dates are transformed by shifting all dates for a patient back by the same random number of days. We describe an issue in which nonspecific (ie, excess) transformation of nondate, date-like text by this deidentification process enables reidentification of all dates, including birthdates, for certain patients. This issue undercuts the common assumption that excess deidentification is a safe tradeoff to protect patient privacy. We present this issue as a caution to other institutions that may also be considering releasing deidentified notes for research.  © 2022 The Author(s). Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the American Medical Informatics Association. All rights reserved.
